course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 19
title: Hungry, tired, hurt, offended, missing someone
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: After a long game Sashko pants "ya khochu pyty" — literally "I want to drink", which is how Ukrainian says I'm thirsty. In the same way "ya khochu yisty" is I'm hungry and "ya khochu spAty" is I'm sleepy.
explain: When he falls, "meni bolyt" means it hurts, and the first answer is "de?", where?
explain: Some words need a gentle voice: "ya obrAzyvsya" means he is hurt by what you said, and you should say "proBAch!" and mean it.
explain: Many Ukrainian families have grandparents and cousins far away, some in danger; when Sashko says "ya skuchAyu", I miss them, be kind and don't joke.
explain: His mum may say "vse budE dobre", everything will be fine, or "bezpEchno", it's safe — and kids shout "zhakh!" for anything from bad news to a spelling test.
fact: "vse budE dobre" means "everything will be fine"; families say it a lot, including about the war back home.
fact: "ya khochu pyty" means "I'm thirsty", literally "I want to drink".
fact: "ya khochu yisty" means "I'm hungry", literally "I want to eat", and kids say it more often than "ya hoLOdnyi".
fact: "ya khochu spAty" means "I'm sleepy", literally "I want to sleep", as at a sleepover late at night.
fact: "meni bolyt" means "it hurts", and the usual answer is "de?", where?; "meni bolyt noha" means my leg or foot hurts.
fact: "ya obrAzyvsya" means "I'm offended, you've hurt my feelings"; the answer is "proBAch!", and it should be taken seriously.
fact: "ne plAch" means "don't cry" and is said to comfort a smaller child; calling someone a "plaksa", a crybaby, is unkind.
fact: "smIshno!" means "funny!" and is said when laughing at a joke.
fact: "yak klAsno!" means "how great! how nice!" and is said on hearing good news.
fact: "ya skuchAyu" means "I miss (someone)", for example grandparents in Ukraine; it is a sensitive topic, so answer gently.
fact: "bezpEchno" means "it's safe", a word parents use to reassure, and a word the family may use about war news.
fact: "zhakh!" means "oh no! how awful!"; kids use it for small things too, like a test tomorrow.
exercise: Sashko's mum hugs him and says "vse budE dobre". What does she mean? | Everything will be fine | Everything is your fault | Everyone go home now | Everything is ready to eat
exercise: After football Sashko gasps "ya khochu pyty". What does he need? | A drink | A sandwich | A nap | A plaster
exercise: Straight after school Sashko moans "ya khochu yisty". What's wrong? | He is hungry | He is thirsty | He is sleepy | He is bored
exercise: At a sleepover, at midnight, Sashko yawns "ya khochu spAty". What does he want? | To go to sleep | To play another game | Something to eat | To go home
exercise: Sashko falls off his scooter and says "meni bolyt". What is the first thing to ask? | de? | chomu? | SKILky? | krutO?
exercise: What does "meni bolyt" mean? | It hurts | I'm bored | I'm angry | I'm lost
exercise: You make a mean joke and Sashko goes quiet: "ya obrAzyvsya". What should you say? | proBAch! | smIshno! | yak klAsno! | molodEts!
exercise: Sashko's little sister falls over and starts to cry. What do you say? | ne plAch | ne zlYsya | ya znAyu! | zhakh!
exercise: You tell a great joke and Sashko laughs: "smIshno!". What does he mean? | Funny! | Weird! | Scary! | Boring!
exercise: Sashko hears you're coming to his party. He says "yak klAsno!". What does he mean? | How great! | How classy! | What a class! | How sad!
exercise: Sashko says quietly "ya skuchAyu za babUsyeyu". What is he telling you? | He misses his granny | He is bored with his granny | He is angry with his granny | He is visiting his granny today
exercise: How should you answer when Sashko says "ya skuchAyu"? | Gently, and without joking | With a joke to cheer him up | By changing the subject loudly | By saying "smIshno!"
exercise: His mum reads the news and says "bezpEchno". What does she mean? | It's safe | It's dangerous | It's late | It's finished
exercise: Sashko hears "zavtra kontrOlna" and shouts "zhakh!". What does he mean? | Oh no! | Hooray! | Cool! | Hello!
exercise: Sashko's cousin hasn't eaten all day. What would he most likely say? | ya khochu yisty | ya khochu spAty | ya khochu pyty | ya vtomYvsya
test: mate says "ya khochu pyty" means "I want to pee" lol. right? | No, it means I'm thirsty | Yes, that's what it means | No, it means I'm hungry | Yes, but only kids say it
test: Sashko says "ya obrAzyvsya" and your mate says "just laugh, it's a joke word". What's true? | It means he is really hurt, so say sorry | It means he thought it was funny | It means he wants to play | It means he is tired
test: Sashko is quiet after his mum talked to his granny far away. You don't know why. What is a good first thing to do? | Ask "shcho stAlosya?" kindly | Tell a loud joke straight away | Say "smIshno!" | Tell him he's being a plaksa
test: Your mate thinks "zhakh!" is only for terrible disasters. What is true? | Kids use it for small things too | It is only said about wars | It is a swear word | It means "great!"
test: What would happen if you said "smIshno!" when Sashko told you his dog had died? | It would sound cruel, like you were laughing | It would comfort him | He would think you said sorry | Nothing, it means "how sad"
test: Sashko's mum says "vse budE dobre" after scary news. Why do families say it so much? | To comfort each other, especially in hard times | Because it means "time for dinner" | Because it is a greeting like hello | Because it is a prayer said at bedtime only
test: Sashko is limping and says "meni bolyt noha". What's going on? | His leg or foot hurts | He is bored of walking | He is hungry | He lost his shoe
test: In a game the losing kid says "ya obrAzyvsya" and walks off. Which answer fixes things best? | proBAch, I didn't mean it | ha, plaksa! | yak klAsno! | zhakh!
test: bloody hot day, you've run around for an hour. What would Sashko most likely say? | ya khochu pyty | ya khochu spAty | ne plAch | bezpEchno
test: Sashko hears his cousin is coming to stay. Which reply fits? | yak klAsno! | meni bolyt | ya obrAzyvsya | ne plAch
test: Your mate says "bezpEchno" means "dangerous" because it sounds scary. What does it really mean? | It's safe | It's dangerous | It's forbidden | It's broken
exam: Determine the meaning of the utterance "ya khochu pyty" when rendered literally and in its customary sense. | Literally "I want to drink", meaning "I am thirsty" | Literally "I want to eat", meaning "I am hungry" | Literally "I want to sleep", meaning "I am sleepy" | Literally "I want to sing", meaning "I am cheerful"
exam: It is given that a companion declares "ya obrAzyvsya". Identify the response most consistent with good conduct. | An apology, "proBAch!" | An exclamation of amusement, "smIshno!" | An exclamation of delight, "yak klAsno!" | An instruction, "ne plAch"
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression "ya skuchAyu"? | It expresses the speaker's longing for an absent person and warrants a gentle response | It expresses boredom and warrants a proposal of a game | It expresses hunger and warrants the offer of food | It expresses anger and warrants a reprimand
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the exclamation "zhakh!". | It expresses dismay and is employed by children even for minor matters | It expresses delight and is reserved for festivals | It is a formal greeting addressed to elders | It is an obscene expression prohibited in households
